{
  "concept": "The moment of inertia of a uniform disk is found by slicing it into thin concentric rings of radius r and width dr, each ring carrying all its mass dm at one distance from the rotation axis, and summing their r²·dm contributions.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "face-on view (left): blue-shaded circular disk with center origin",
    "face-on axes: x pointing up, y pointing right, z labeled at center (axis out of page)",
    "orange annular ring of radius r and width dr, concentric with the disk",
    "double-headed arrow labeled R from center to outer rim",
    "arrow labeled r from center to the orange ring",
    "short arrow labeled dr indicating the ring's thickness",
    "perspective view (right): blue-shaded ellipse (tilted disk) with orange rim",
    "perspective axes: z vertical, y to the right, x toward the lower left",
    "curved rotation arrow looping around the z axis",
    "arrow labeled r from center to a mass element on the ring",
    "small highlighted mass element labeled dm on the ring",
    "contribution indicator showing r²·dm growing with r"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "ringRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Ring radius r",
      "range": [0.1, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0.6,
      "teaches": "A ring's contribution to moment of inertia grows as r² — mass farther from the axis counts disproportionately more, which is why the disk's I is less than if all mass sat at the rim"
    },
    {
      "id": "ringWidth",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Ring width dr",
      "range": [0.02, 0.25, 0.01],
      "default": 0.08,
      "teaches": "The ring's mass dm is proportional to its area 2πr·dr — the differential strip supplies the mass element being summed in the integral"
    },
    {
      "id": "viewToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Face-on", "Perspective"],
      "default": "Face-on",
      "teaches": "The same annular integration element appears as a concentric ring face-on and as a tilted band around the rotation axis in perspective — it is one full ring at a single radius r, not a patch"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Slice the disk into rings",
      "narration": "The disk is decomposed into thin concentric rings. The highlighted orange ring sits at radius r with thickness dr — every bit of its mass is the same distance from the axis.",
      "state": { "ringRadius": 0.6, "ringWidth": 0.08, "viewToggle": "Face-on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the ring outward",
      "narration": "Drag r toward the rim: the ring's r²·dm contribution indicator grows quadratically. Mass near the edge contributes far more to the moment of inertia than the same mass near the center.",
      "state": { "ringRadius": 0.95, "ringWidth": 0.08, "viewToggle": "Face-on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Widen the strip",
      "narration": "Increase dr and watch the shaded strip area — and hence dm — grow. In the limit dr → 0 the ring becomes the differential mass element dm = (2M/R²) r dr inside the integral.",
      "state": { "ringRadius": 0.6, "ringWidth": 0.22, "viewToggle": "Face-on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "See it in perspective",
      "narration": "Switch to the tilted view: the same ring wraps around the vertical z axis as the disk spins, and the dm marker rides the ring — confirming the integration element is a full annulus at one radius.",
      "state": { "ringRadius": 0.6, "ringWidth": 0.08, "viewToggle": "Perspective" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because every point of a thin ring is the same distance r from the axis, its moment of inertia is simply r²dm — summing these ring contributions from r=0 to r=R yields the disk's I = ½MR².",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two side-by-side panels sharing one highlighted annulus. Left panel: flat blue-lavender filled circle, black axes (x up, y right), 'z' labeled at the center dot, orange ring of adjustable radius and thickness, arrows labeled R (to rim), r (to ring), dr (ring thickness). Right panel: same disk drawn as a flattened ellipse in oblique view with orange rim, z axis vertical with a curved rotation arrow around it, y to the right, x angled down-left, a small dark mass element labeled dm sitting on the ring with an r arrow from center to it. Use a warm orange for the ring in both views and keep the two views synchronized: changing r or dr updates both panels simultaneously. Add a small readout near the ring showing dm and r²·dm values updating with the sliders."
}

